100
00:06:18,278 --> 00:06:21,588
So th-this forms the four pillars
of building your enterprise AI.

101
00:06:22,858 --> 00:06:27,098
I'll double-click in each of
them one by one, and yeah.

102
00:06:28,738 --> 00:06:29,848
Starting with the context.

103
00:06:30,218 --> 00:06:32,677
Context is the king in the LLM era.

104
00:06:34,618 --> 00:06:38,818
The context challenge in enterprise AI,
which we also face in Harness because

105
00:06:39,138 --> 00:06:42,548
Harness has a lot of product modules.

106
00:06:42,608 --> 00:06:44,038
We-- I just talked about CI.

107
00:06:44,408 --> 00:06:45,928
CI is one big product.

108
00:06:46,348 --> 00:06:47,648
CD is another big product.

109
00:06:48,238 --> 00:06:53,658
Then we also let folks create PRs
just like you create PRs on GitHub.

110
00:06:54,198 --> 00:06:55,568
So things are siloed.

111
00:06:55,728 --> 00:06:59,428
There is siloed data everywhere,
information is trapped in different

112
00:06:59,428 --> 00:07:04,588
systems, information is trapped in
siloed Confluence, some siloed databases

113
00:07:04,688 --> 00:07:07,528
which the entire AI is not aware about.

114
00:07:08,678 --> 00:07:10,148
Then lack of domain knowledge.

115
00:07:10,828 --> 00:07:11,738
LLMs are generic.

116
00:07:11,738 --> 00:07:15,528
They are trained on all the data in
the world, so they don't understand

117
00:07:15,538 --> 00:07:20,388
the specific jargons or the
processes you have in your ecosystem.

118
00:07:20,798 --> 00:07:27,808
So CI in Harness could mean the CI term
in DevOps could very well mean different

119
00:07:28,008 --> 00:07:30,217
as a CI term in different ecosystem.

120
00:07:30,678 --> 00:07:33,967
So LLMs don't understand your
company's specific jargons or

121
00:07:33,968 --> 00:07:35,198
your company's specific products.

122
00:07:35,258 --> 00:07:35,948
They are generic.

123
00:07:37,368 --> 00:07:39,598
Third is risk of hallu-hallucinations.

124
00:07:39,858 --> 00:07:42,117
A-AI, you have to ground your AI.

125
00:07:42,538 --> 00:07:47,348
Otherwise it will hallucinate and generate
misleading information very confidently.

126
00:07:48,648 --> 00:07:51,458
And the fourth, as I mentioned,
the security and privacy risk.

127
00:07:51,538 --> 00:07:55,618
Sharing sensitive data with external
models is risky and could lead

128
00:07:55,648 --> 00:07:57,598
to, say, significant threats.

129
00:07:59,008 --> 00:08:02,738
So how do you solve all of
these problems in the context?

130
00:08:04,478 --> 00:08:06,068
As I said, context is the new code.

131
00:08:06,498 --> 00:08:09,878
So you start with, of course,
organizational intelligence.

132
00:08:09,928 --> 00:08:16,598
You consolidate different data,
different intelligence you have siloed in

133
00:08:16,598 --> 00:08:18,888
different places into a unified platform.

134
00:08:19,758 --> 00:08:23,738
AI should be able to query different
data you have across the board

135
00:08:24,078 --> 00:08:26,298
and not think of AI as silos.

136
00:08:28,634 --> 00:08:30,454
The s-second part, how do you do that?

137
00:08:30,854 --> 00:08:33,404
You have to think about a knowledge graph.

138
00:08:33,444 --> 00:08:37,794
It's like a living relationship,
living graph between your entities.

139
00:08:38,134 --> 00:08:41,984
To give an example, so when
I'm writing code, I raise a PR.

140
00:08:42,494 --> 00:08:46,884
The PR goes through certain checks,
then it gets deployed, and then it goes

141
00:08:46,884 --> 00:08:48,454
through multiple cycles of production.

142
00:08:49,004 --> 00:08:51,554
So if-- And then certain thing fails.

143
00:08:51,794 --> 00:08:55,644
You get some prod alerts, incidents
PagerDuty on-calls, and all of that.

144
00:08:56,124 --> 00:09:01,914
So if I-- if you want to ask AI a very
basic question, which of my PRs caused

145
00:09:01,954 --> 00:09:03,964
incident in the last month or last week?

146
00:09:04,304 --> 00:09:05,044
How do you do that?

147
00:09:05,074 --> 00:09:08,684
Because it is passing through
four, five different phases.

148
00:09:08,904 --> 00:09:10,514
It is passing through creating a PR.

149
00:09:10,944 --> 00:09:12,824
It is passing through a CI/CD.

150
00:09:13,184 --> 00:09:16,824
The issues could have been
anywhere in terms of what is being

151
00:09:16,824 --> 00:09:19,934
deployed, what version is being
deployed, what commits went into

152
00:09:19,934 --> 00:09:21,854
the changes, and why did it fail.

153
00:09:22,364 --> 00:09:27,214
So if you have a graph, knowledge graph
between all of your data, if you imagine

154
00:09:27,214 --> 00:09:31,714
it as like a graph, AI will really
rock in terms of walking through the

155
00:09:31,724 --> 00:09:34,824
graph and giving you the right answers.

156
00:09:34,994 --> 00:09:37,914
Knowledge graph is one of the
beautiful way to keep your AI grounded.

157
00:09:39,314 --> 00:09:40,474
Third is memories.

158
00:09:40,514 --> 00:09:43,624
Memories power a lot
of the context as well.

159
00:09:44,184 --> 00:09:48,534
You have to teach your AI what you're
doing, and you have to distinguish between

160
00:09:49,294 --> 00:09:50,744
short-term memories, long-term memories.

161
00:09:50,744 --> 00:09:53,304
Memories just like-- work
just like our brain work.

162
00:09:53,374 --> 00:09:55,924
We have short-term memories,
we do have long-term memories.

163
00:09:55,924 --> 00:09:57,714
There are certain things
we remember for long.

164
00:09:58,164 --> 00:10:00,144
There are certain things
we remember for short-term.

165
00:10:01,744 --> 00:10:03,704
And the fourth is this--
fourth is the tools.

166
00:10:03,944 --> 00:10:08,024
Tools are the most… You have heard
about MCPs in the world, and so those

167
00:10:08,024 --> 00:10:09,424
are the tools I'm talking about.

168
00:10:09,824 --> 00:10:11,904
So tools power the context.

169
00:10:12,384 --> 00:10:16,424
Tools are also part of the context in
order for AI to take specific actions,

170
00:10:16,794 --> 00:10:19,154
in order for AI to query more data.

171
00:10:19,774 --> 00:10:24,014
S-So knowledge graph is only-- will
only make sense for the data you have.

172
00:10:24,374 --> 00:10:28,564
But if you still want external data,
let's say from third-party sources,

173
00:10:28,574 --> 00:10:32,334
you would still have to throw
tools at it and get data from it.

174
00:10:33,804 --> 00:10:39,534
And all of this, you have to really
context engineer it well because LLMs

175
00:10:39,924 --> 00:10:43,563
usually have-- we have seen we all know
it's either has a limit of two hundred

176
00:10:43,563 --> 00:10:48,663
K tokens and now we have started to
seeing one million tokens on the LLM.

177
00:10:48,664 --> 00:10:53,573
So it all depends on us how well
information you can fit into

178
00:10:53,573 --> 00:10:55,003
that one million context window.

179
00:10:58,815 --> 00:10:59,155
Okay.

180
00:10:59,225 --> 00:11:02,725
Moving into how do you really
do context engineering?

181
00:11:03,195 --> 00:11:08,145
There are certain few tips and tricks
of doing the right context engineering.

182
00:11:08,545 --> 00:11:13,505
The first one is the most obvious one,
which a lot of the AI agents actually do.

183
00:11:14,065 --> 00:11:14,725
So

184
00:11:18,531 --> 00:11:21,521
So imagine like you're talking
to an AI chatbot, like ChatGPT

185
00:11:21,521 --> 00:11:23,112
or a CloudCode or anybody.

186
00:11:23,431 --> 00:11:26,021
You are talking, you are conversing,
you're asking some questions,

187
00:11:26,021 --> 00:11:27,141
and the context is growing.

188
00:11:27,581 --> 00:11:31,211
And the context is almost about to hit
eighty percent of the context window.

189
00:11:31,721 --> 00:11:36,461
What they do is they summarize the
entire conversation, they compact it.

190
00:11:36,961 --> 00:11:40,651
They take the key decisions out from it,
whatever you have been talking about.

191
00:11:41,011 --> 00:11:42,691
Think of it like a garbage collector.

192
00:11:43,171 --> 00:11:45,741
Like, whatever the garbage
you don't need, throw it away.

193
00:11:45,811 --> 00:11:48,741
What is the important pieces
you need in your conversation,

194
00:11:48,761 --> 00:11:50,132
you keep it in the context.

195
00:11:50,541 --> 00:11:54,211
And then once you summarize, it
goes back again to, let's say,

196
00:11:54,581 --> 00:11:57,571
twenty K tokens to thirty K tokens
de-depending on your summary

197
00:11:59,865 --> 00:12:04,256
Second is the major
contributor of context bloat.

198
00:12:04,736 --> 00:12:10,915
So giving an example, let's say
you have connected GitHub or

199
00:12:10,915 --> 00:12:13,636
Jira Atlassian to your AI agent.

200
00:12:14,265 --> 00:12:18,575
Now, you have asked it, "Can you show
me a list of Jira I worked in last

201
00:12:18,575 --> 00:12:23,085
month, and what is the status for it?"
It will go make a Jira API call, Jira

202
00:12:23,085 --> 00:12:28,386
MCP call, and give you a huge JSON list
of these are all the Jiras, these are

203
00:12:28,386 --> 00:12:31,356
all the Jira description, these are
all the Jira comments, and all of it.

204
00:12:32,006 --> 00:12:35,365
If you throw the entire output in
the context window, it's not going

205
00:12:35,365 --> 00:12:38,525
to work at all because you just
took up the entire context window.

206
00:12:39,005 --> 00:12:45,845
So there are certain tips and tricks
to not throw it in the context window,

207
00:12:45,885 --> 00:12:48,645
rather throw it into the external files.

208
00:12:49,045 --> 00:12:52,555
So whenever you are getting a large
output, dump it into the external

209
00:12:52,555 --> 00:12:57,725
files, throw the agent to go and
read those files line by line or

210
00:12:57,735 --> 00:13:01,175
certain sections of the line, rather
than reading the entire file again.

211
00:13:01,786 --> 00:13:05,846
And the agents are again amazing
at reading the certain specific

212
00:13:05,856 --> 00:13:07,896
portions and reason about why.

213
00:13:09,085 --> 00:13:13,285
For example, I am analyzing
a big stack trace.

214
00:13:13,386 --> 00:13:15,396
So I'll just throw it in a file.

215
00:13:15,815 --> 00:13:19,375
And I have asked agent, "Can you
look at the error logs, and can you

216
00:13:19,375 --> 00:13:22,795
tell me what is happening? Can you
summarize? Can you give me an RCA?"

217
00:13:23,455 --> 00:13:27,465
The agents, the LLMs are smart to
look at what part of the file to

218
00:13:27,466 --> 00:13:28,726
read instead of the entire file.

219
00:13:29,195 --> 00:13:34,005
So just insert summaries or some semantic
references in the context window.

220
00:13:34,006 --> 00:13:37,145
Don't dump the large output in
the context window directly.

221
00:13:38,716 --> 00:13:41,216
The third piece is an interesting one.

222
00:13:41,315 --> 00:13:44,075
It came from Anthropic, context editing.

223
00:13:45,275 --> 00:13:47,565
It is a proactive approach for compaction.

224
00:13:47,825 --> 00:13:52,345
So what I mean by that is, let's
suppose you have a repository.

225
00:13:52,555 --> 00:13:57,035
You have asked it to-- You have
asked the Claude code or Cursor to,

226
00:13:57,235 --> 00:14:00,445
"Can you go and build and test this
repository?" Just a basic question.

227
00:14:01,135 --> 00:14:03,615
Now, the agent will figure it out.

228
00:14:03,645 --> 00:14:07,135
It may-- Let's say it's
doing a Gradle build.

229
00:14:07,135 --> 00:14:08,475
It's figuring out the right command.

230
00:14:09,065 --> 00:14:11,655
The g-- And it ran the
command, it errored out.

231
00:14:11,855 --> 00:14:13,105
Then it figured it out again.

232
00:14:13,145 --> 00:14:16,155
"Okay, I need this argument."
Then it errored out.

233
00:14:16,245 --> 00:14:18,405
It, And then it is figuring
out the right command again.

234
00:14:19,075 --> 00:14:23,105
So you don't need all of the
things you have just done above

235
00:14:23,195 --> 00:14:27,235
because those are not the right
commands to build the repository.

236
00:14:27,635 --> 00:14:31,425
So you keep on removing irrelevant
context as you progress from the

237
00:14:31,425 --> 00:14:35,375
context so that you proactively keep
your context healthy rather than always

238
00:14:35,375 --> 00:14:36,865
relying on compaction at the end.

239
00:14:38,807 --> 00:14:41,267
And the last piece is the
semantic augmentation.

240
00:14:41,477 --> 00:14:45,247
If you have also heard of
skills which which again came

241
00:14:45,247 --> 00:14:46,557
from Anthropic as a standard.

242
00:14:47,007 --> 00:14:51,477
So you don't really have to throw all the
context at once in the context window.

243
00:14:51,867 --> 00:14:59,157
You let LLM semantically look for those
skills or content as it goes along,

244
00:14:59,237 --> 00:15:02,537
rather than dumping all of it in the
system prompt, rather than dumping

245
00:15:02,537 --> 00:15:03,877
all of it in the context window.

246
00:15:05,907 --> 00:15:10,387
And why it matters, it optimizes your
token costs because you are very--

247
00:15:11,257 --> 00:15:15,017
you're very smart about what you are
putting into the context versus what

248
00:15:15,017 --> 00:15:16,507
you're not putting into the context.

249
00:15:16,507 --> 00:15:20,857
You're very smart about progressively
semantically augmenting things in

250
00:15:20,867 --> 00:15:24,357
the context window, rather than
just dumping everything in the

251
00:15:24,357 --> 00:15:28,327
context window and saying the LLM
is bad or the AI is hallucinating.

252
00:15:29,617 --> 00:15:35,267
And these, if you follow like even if
you ignore context editing, if you follow

253
00:15:35,267 --> 00:15:36,887
compaction, that's the easiest one.

254
00:15:37,177 --> 00:15:40,487
The large outputs, dumping
them into the files and just

255
00:15:40,487 --> 00:15:42,127
let agent look at those files.

256
00:15:42,617 --> 00:15:44,897
And the last piece, the
semantic augmentation.

257
00:15:45,487 --> 00:15:47,837
You would definitely see an improvement.

258
00:15:48,457 --> 00:15:51,247
I can even claim you can definitely
see an improvement like eighty to

259
00:15:51,247 --> 00:15:54,617
ninety percent in terms of accuracy
in your token co-- token costs

260
00:15:58,163 --> 00:16:00,263
How do you go about
building a knowledge graph?

261
00:16:00,443 --> 00:16:04,683
As I just mentioned, you-- the
data is present at multiple places.

262
00:16:04,703 --> 00:16:08,753
It is present in code, it is present
in your docs, it is present in your

263
00:16:08,753 --> 00:16:12,213
logs, it is present in your Jira
somewhere in Confluence documents.

264
00:16:12,553 --> 00:16:14,993
You maintain your configs
at a different repo.

265
00:16:15,643 --> 00:16:16,923
So what do you do?

266
00:16:16,933 --> 00:16:20,023
You sit down, you figure out
what are all the entities I care

267
00:16:20,033 --> 00:16:22,073
about in my entire ecosystem.

268
00:16:22,593 --> 00:16:26,183
I care about services,
I care about my users.

269
00:16:26,303 --> 00:16:27,713
Depends from use case to use case.

270
00:16:28,133 --> 00:16:29,473
I care about my incidents.

271
00:16:31,263 --> 00:16:34,643
Then you enrich those entities
with metadata, like who is

272
00:16:34,643 --> 00:16:35,703
the owner for the entity.

273
00:16:35,713 --> 00:16:38,963
Again, it depends on your use case,
but the idea is you extract entities

274
00:16:38,973 --> 00:16:41,433
first as a first-class thing.

275
00:16:41,563 --> 00:16:44,413
Then you enrich those entities
with certain metadata.

276
00:16:45,223 --> 00:16:50,403
Then you make a graph out of those
entities, like a user is linked to

277
00:16:50,403 --> 00:16:54,443
a service, or a user is linked to
an incident, or a particular release

278
00:16:54,453 --> 00:16:56,533
version is in linked to an incident.

279
00:16:56,823 --> 00:17:03,353
So you can have a graph who owns what,
what depends on what, and just expose

280
00:17:03,353 --> 00:17:04,443
it via Graphi-API to the AI agents.

281
00:17:04,443 --> 00:17:04,923
It co-- So it will be able to

282
00:17:08,263 --> 00:17:13,253
then query the entire graph, walk through
the graph, filter down a sub-graph,

283
00:17:13,753 --> 00:17:15,783
get the data, and then work on it.

284
00:17:16,233 --> 00:17:20,563
If you don't have knowledge graph,
what you would be doing is, let's

285
00:17:20,573 --> 00:17:24,743
say you didn't have knowledge graph,
there were three data sources.

286
00:17:26,323 --> 00:17:29,603
The AI agent will look
at first data source.

287
00:17:29,763 --> 00:17:31,583
It will have huge amount of data.

288
00:17:32,143 --> 00:17:34,003
Then it will look at second source.

289
00:17:34,173 --> 00:17:36,003
It will again have huge amount of data.

290
00:17:36,413 --> 00:17:40,483
The agent will try to link the
relationships on there itself, and

291
00:17:40,483 --> 00:17:44,543
they might hallucinate, because again,
they don't understand the relationships

292
00:17:44,543 --> 00:17:46,013
which you have in your ecosystem.

293
00:17:46,323 --> 00:17:47,383
They are generic LLMs.

294
00:17:47,403 --> 00:17:49,613
They don't understand your
organization knowledge.

295
00:17:50,303 --> 00:17:51,663
So this is the most critical part.

296
00:17:51,693 --> 00:17:56,043
If you have an ecosystem where
your data is siloed and data

297
00:17:56,043 --> 00:17:59,163
is at at different data sources

298
00:18:02,245 --> 00:18:05,455
If context is a new code, knowledge
graph is the compiler for it.

299
00:18:05,555 --> 00:18:06,755
So yeah

300
00:18:09,459 --> 00:18:11,729
Moving on to personalization.

301
00:18:13,319 --> 00:18:16,779
Memories is the only key
piece for personalization.

302
00:18:16,779 --> 00:18:21,759
It gets its-- get its own space from
context because it needs its dedica--

303
00:18:21,809 --> 00:18:26,809
it needs a dedicated investment
in itself to power memories.

304
00:18:26,809 --> 00:18:29,489
And there are different types of memories.

305
00:18:29,829 --> 00:18:33,349
There is short-term memory, like
the one we are just doing as a call.

306
00:18:33,699 --> 00:18:37,219
You guys might remember what I
just said for a short term, but

307
00:18:37,219 --> 00:18:40,829
you will take away some long-term
memories as a part of this meetup.

308
00:18:41,179 --> 00:18:43,246
So think of it from that an-that angle.

309
00:18:43,246 --> 00:18:47,159
Short-term memory is what you're
able to retain as you go along in

310
00:18:47,159 --> 00:18:48,869
the AI when you're chatting with AI.

311
00:18:49,409 --> 00:18:53,439
Long-term memories is when you
start bubbling up the short-term

312
00:18:53,439 --> 00:18:54,929
memories into long-term memories.

313
00:18:54,939 --> 00:19:00,339
For example, this user asks a lot of
questions about why are my logs failing.

314
00:19:00,789 --> 00:19:02,889
This user actively works on GitHub.

315
00:19:03,389 --> 00:19:06,659
This team actively works on these repos.

316
00:19:07,019 --> 00:19:11,499
So you can bubble up some of the
memories as long-term memories and

317
00:19:14,299 --> 00:19:19,019
as you as I mentioned out, chatbots
forget, assistants remember.

318
00:19:19,079 --> 00:19:22,519
That's why we call it AI
assistant, not really AI chatbot.

319
00:19:23,209 --> 00:19:25,809
Memories give you multiple things.

320
00:19:25,839 --> 00:19:28,779
It powers your context,
it maintains continuity.

321
00:19:28,819 --> 00:19:31,739
It does not start again from fresh.

322
00:19:31,789 --> 00:19:37,189
It already remembers what you have
done before, and that brings it-- that

323
00:19:37,199 --> 00:19:41,849
brings in confidence to your end users
who are using the AI you have built.

324
00:19:42,709 --> 00:19:46,989
And context makes AI relevant,
memory makes it human.

325
00:19:47,879 --> 00:19:50,909
It makes it feel like it is
remembering, and that's what

326
00:19:50,909 --> 00:19:52,189
we humans are also amazing at

327
00:19:58,777 --> 00:19:59,147
Yeah.

328
00:19:59,407 --> 00:20:02,577
Chatbots talk, assistants collaborate.

329
00:20:03,147 --> 00:20:09,107
So imagine like you are, let's say,
building an AI chat for your ecosystem.

330
00:20:09,897 --> 00:20:13,457
If you just throw in an AI chat
as a pop-up on the right, which

331
00:20:13,467 --> 00:20:16,157
you might have mostly seen in
different products, now there's an

332
00:20:16,157 --> 00:20:17,857
AI chat pop up on the bottom right.

333
00:20:18,537 --> 00:20:24,927
And if it is just working independently,
it is not understanding what screen

334
00:20:24,927 --> 00:20:27,527
you are on, where are you navigating.

335
00:20:28,167 --> 00:20:34,107
And if it is just acting independently,
users will feel out of place that I am

336
00:20:34,107 --> 00:20:38,917
on this particular page of this product,
why is my AI not talking about this page?

337
00:20:39,867 --> 00:20:45,987
Taking it to the next level, you should
also think about it as an assistant.

338
00:20:46,227 --> 00:20:55,647
If I am on a certain page, for example,
I want to generate a CI pipeline, CI for

339
00:20:55,717 --> 00:21:00,177
my code, and I am on a CI screen where
I'm building the pipeline, I'm writing

340
00:21:00,177 --> 00:21:03,997
unit tests, I'm writing integration
tests, I'm writing security test.

341
00:21:05,027 --> 00:21:09,397
You-- It would be amazing if you
are able to ask the AI chat, "Can

342
00:21:09,397 --> 00:21:13,637
you create a CI pipeline for me and
validate if it is working or not?

343
00:21:14,357 --> 00:21:14,967
Run it.

344
00:21:15,267 --> 00:21:17,247
If it errors out, fix it.

345
00:21:17,787 --> 00:21:23,207
Try to fig-- run it again." And if it
is able to do it interactively in your

346
00:21:23,637 --> 00:21:28,107
AI app, that brings a lot of power
when you expose AI to the customer.

347
00:21:28,107 --> 00:21:33,657
So think of AI not as an independent
thing in your product, but

348
00:21:33,687 --> 00:21:34,707
rather it should collaborate.

349
00:21:34,747 --> 00:21:39,177
Just like-- Again, like when you write
code with Cursor or Cloud Code, it--

350
00:21:39,457 --> 00:21:41,477
you see what the code is writing.

351
00:21:41,977 --> 00:21:46,537
The AI chat is also able to understand
what the changes it is making, what

352
00:21:46,537 --> 00:21:48,227
the changes you are making in the code.

353
00:21:48,537 --> 00:21:53,967
So I'm just painting that
anal-analogy on any of the products

354
00:21:53,967 --> 00:21:56,017
you want to throw the AI on it.

355
00:21:56,487 --> 00:21:58,517
So basically, the idea is it
should be able to take actions

356
00:21:58,537 --> 00:22:02,677
back in the app, understand the app
context, and then further go along.

357
00:22:05,665 --> 00:22:07,805
And that's how we have seen
the AI world evolve, right?

358
00:22:07,805 --> 00:22:11,945
It's in last year, last to last year
when all of it started it as a chatbot.

359
00:22:12,375 --> 00:22:17,425
But then we saw the evaluation,
e-evolution of AI from AI chatbot to AI

360
00:22:17,425 --> 00:22:23,085
assistants when it is able to take actions
in your app, and it is able to go along

361
00:22:25,567 --> 00:22:31,337
And the third piece of the evolution
of AI is the autonomous agent.

362
00:22:31,387 --> 00:22:35,317
So I'll again walk through the
evolution of AI since we have

363
00:22:35,317 --> 00:22:37,537
seen it from last couple of years.

364
00:22:37,987 --> 00:22:40,647
Started with a chatbot,
started with the basic prompts.

365
00:22:40,827 --> 00:22:46,667
It handles basic single predefined
tasks, no, no good context or awareness.

366
00:22:47,177 --> 00:22:49,057
Then it went into assistant mode.

367
00:22:49,797 --> 00:22:51,887
It is able to understand
what you're talking about.

368
00:22:51,887 --> 00:22:55,557
It is able to take guided actions
within work-- within your workflow.

369
00:22:56,317 --> 00:23:00,847
And in the current era, in the current
past few months, AI has also started

370
00:23:00,847 --> 00:23:03,047
evolving into an autonomous agents.

371
00:23:03,337 --> 00:23:09,787
So might have heard of Claude Cowork or
Cursor agents, where it is like there is

372
00:23:09,787 --> 00:23:11,497
no human also interacting with the AI.

373
00:23:11,537 --> 00:23:13,317
It's like it's listening on some trigger.

374
00:23:14,257 --> 00:23:19,447
It starts working on it, takes some
actions, maybe or may not be wait on

375
00:23:19,447 --> 00:23:22,397
your approval, and just get done with it.

376
00:23:22,397 --> 00:23:26,957
So for example, I have an autonomous
agent which runs every day at ten

377
00:23:26,957 --> 00:23:31,627
AM, looks at my Slack, looks at my
email, looks at my Jira, everything.

378
00:23:31,987 --> 00:23:36,927
Figures out what all messages should
I look at, what is my priority to look

379
00:23:36,927 --> 00:23:40,867
at today versus not look at later in
the day, what actions should I take.

380
00:23:41,287 --> 00:23:45,307
And it is an autonomous agent which runs
every day at ten AM and just gives me a

381
00:23:45,307 --> 00:23:49,727
nice page of what should I look at today
versus what are my priorities for today.

382
00:23:51,007 --> 00:23:55,987
A second example for autonomous
agent could be, I keep coming back

383
00:23:55,987 --> 00:23:59,977
to the example of raising a PR
and there is a CI running in it.

384
00:24:00,277 --> 00:24:03,207
So imagine you as a developer
are working on a code.

385
00:24:03,597 --> 00:24:04,907
You raise a PR for it.

386
00:24:05,577 --> 00:24:09,557
Now the check starts running,
the unit test, the integration

387
00:24:09,557 --> 00:24:12,977
test, the security test, and they
do take their own sweet time.

388
00:24:12,977 --> 00:24:15,657
They do take twenty minutes,
thirty minutes to run, depending

389
00:24:15,657 --> 00:24:17,207
on how heavy are your tests.

390
00:24:17,207 --> 00:24:21,847
But you cannot-- You are not waiting for
twenty, thirty minutes and just looking

391
00:24:21,847 --> 00:24:23,857
at it and waiting for it to fail or pass.

392
00:24:24,287 --> 00:24:25,817
You go and pick up a different work.

393
00:24:26,777 --> 00:24:29,357
Now, when it fails, you get notified.

394
00:24:29,807 --> 00:24:33,627
You have to stop your work, come
back, go and fix your failures,

395
00:24:34,167 --> 00:24:36,468
raise and-- raise the fix again.

396
00:24:36,597 --> 00:24:37,487
Again, wait for it.

397
00:24:37,877 --> 00:24:39,167
So that's very tedious.

398
00:24:39,217 --> 00:24:42,637
That's like a toil in your usual workflow.

399
00:24:43,107 --> 00:24:48,127
Imagine there was an autonomous agent
where you just raise a PR, the CI is

400
00:24:48,127 --> 00:24:53,297
running, and the agent is listening
and keeping it-- keeping an eye on your

401
00:24:53,347 --> 00:24:55,297
PR, whether the CI is failing or not.

402
00:24:55,667 --> 00:24:58,567
If it is failing, it is
automatically figuring out what

403
00:24:58,567 --> 00:25:02,237
are the fixes needed, committing
it, making sure your PR is green.

404
00:25:02,567 --> 00:25:05,027
So you don't really have
to do anything else.

405
00:25:05,057 --> 00:25:08,698
You just focus on your next work and let
the autonomous agents take care of it.

406
00:25:08,707 --> 00:25:10,387
So that's what I'm talking about here.

407
00:25:12,307 --> 00:25:16,347
And yeah, coming with a quote again,
"If assistants are copilots, autonomous

408
00:25:16,347 --> 00:25:18,057
agents are like your teammates."

409
00:25:21,779 --> 00:25:22,079
Okay.

410
00:25:22,189 --> 00:25:25,209
Coming to the most important
piece for doing AI.

411
00:25:25,709 --> 00:25:31,639
This is like after context, I would not
even keep memories as a second thing.

412
00:25:31,699 --> 00:25:36,759
I would keep evals as the second most
important thing to focus on for your AI.

413
00:25:36,819 --> 00:25:37,669
What are evals?

414
00:25:39,009 --> 00:25:44,939
Evals are just like your unit tests for
AI, but there is a difference between

415
00:25:45,609 --> 00:25:49,459
how you were writing unit tests or
integration tests for your normal software

416
00:25:49,969 --> 00:25:51,759
because that was a deterministic code.

417
00:25:51,959 --> 00:25:56,199
You know what happens next, you know
what the code is going to do next.

418
00:25:56,209 --> 00:26:01,309
So you're-- you were able to write
a better deterministic unit tests or

419
00:26:01,699 --> 00:26:04,089
integration tests on top of your software.

420
00:26:04,629 --> 00:26:06,009
In AI, it's not the case.

421
00:26:06,029 --> 00:26:07,669
It is not deterministic.

422
00:26:07,849 --> 00:26:09,909
A lot of the outputs are probabilistic.

423
00:26:09,969 --> 00:26:13,369
So the traditional unit
test mindsets don't apply.

424
00:26:14,819 --> 00:26:20,309
And we have seen like most teams skip
evals because it is very hard to define

425
00:26:20,309 --> 00:26:22,499
what good looks like for an AI agent.

426
00:26:22,599 --> 00:26:24,799
How do you know what is
good versus what is bad?

427
00:26:25,589 --> 00:26:29,409
But without any kind of test,
I'm not even talking about AI.

428
00:26:29,429 --> 00:26:32,159
Imagine you're writing a software
system, but you're not writing

429
00:26:32,159 --> 00:26:33,769
tests at all, any sorts of tests.

430
00:26:34,419 --> 00:26:39,119
You are just throwing some code to
the customer where you don't even

431
00:26:39,119 --> 00:26:43,079
know what's going to happen, and it--
your users are just going to throw

432
00:26:43,079 --> 00:26:47,379
away your product quickly because
it's not working as you expected.

433
00:26:48,749 --> 00:26:54,119
So eval's mindset is the most
important mindset to have when

434
00:26:54,119 --> 00:26:55,919
you are building any AI ecosystem.

435
00:26:56,389 --> 00:26:57,639
It quantifies trust.

436
00:26:58,059 --> 00:27:02,169
It gives you confidence
on how it is doing.

437
00:27:02,559 --> 00:27:06,239
It gives you confidence on when
you are making changes in the code,

438
00:27:06,419 --> 00:27:07,839
there are no regressions going.

439
00:27:08,219 --> 00:27:12,069
And so I'll give you a very basic example.

440
00:27:13,709 --> 00:27:17,569
Let's say I have created an AI agent
which is wor-working very well with

441
00:27:17,609 --> 00:27:21,699
Opus because Opus is one of the most
amazing frontier models in the world.

442
00:27:22,399 --> 00:27:29,009
But I realize that it is burning a lot
of tokens, a lot of cost, and I want

443
00:27:29,009 --> 00:27:32,659
to switch it to Sonnet now, or I want
to switch it to OpenAI for that matter.

444
00:27:33,439 --> 00:27:35,339
How can you confidently
switch it to Sonnet?

445
00:27:35,619 --> 00:27:39,319
You cannot just go and change the
model name and say it will work best.

446
00:27:39,489 --> 00:27:39,959
It won't.

447
00:27:40,609 --> 00:27:44,609
And you cannot improve what
you cannot me-measure, and you

448
00:27:44,639 --> 00:27:46,319
cannot trust what you don't test.

449
00:27:46,419 --> 00:27:48,089
That-- this statement applies everywhere.

450
00:27:48,089 --> 00:27:49,119
It's not just for AI.

451
00:27:49,499 --> 00:27:53,659
I'm just calling it out because
evals is the most critical piece to

452
00:27:53,679 --> 00:27:54,729
think about when you are building AI.

453
00:27:57,401 --> 00:27:58,751
So how to think about evals?

454
00:27:59,261 --> 00:28:02,941
There are different frameworks for evals
out in the world, so pick any of them.

455
00:28:03,001 --> 00:28:06,241
It's not like one is the best
or other, others are not.

456
00:28:06,701 --> 00:28:09,431
But what is the right mental
model to think about AI?

457
00:28:12,221 --> 00:28:18,181
So most of the evals for AI is…
Since AI is non-deterministic, you

458
00:28:18,181 --> 00:28:20,381
cannot write a deterministic test.

459
00:28:20,631 --> 00:28:24,451
You again have to write a
non-deterministic test using another LLM.

460
00:28:25,011 --> 00:28:28,021
So a very basic example is

461
00:28:30,121 --> 00:28:34,701
Let's say you ask your AI what is the
capital of France, and it came up and said

462
00:28:34,711 --> 00:28:36,301
the the capital of France is New Delhi.

463
00:28:36,831 --> 00:28:42,101
So you just throw that entire user--
entire message to a different LLM,

464
00:28:42,301 --> 00:28:44,641
maybe a different LLM, not the same LLM.

465
00:28:44,641 --> 00:28:48,811
So if you're using Opus or Sonnet for
that, let's say you throw it to OpenAI

466
00:28:48,821 --> 00:28:52,001
and ask it, "Is the answer even correct?

467
00:28:52,301 --> 00:28:56,661
Is the answer even grounded?" And it
can come back and say "The answer is

468
00:28:56,661 --> 00:29:00,561
not correct because the capital of
France is Paris and not New Delhi."

469
00:29:00,951 --> 00:29:02,251
That's a very basic example.

470
00:29:02,771 --> 00:29:06,261
There are different eval checks that
are available out in their world.

471
00:29:06,281 --> 00:29:12,261
They check for if your AI is
emitting any toxic answers in your

472
00:29:12,261 --> 00:29:17,631
response, if your AI is drifting
from what you have expected it to.

473
00:29:17,781 --> 00:29:25,031
So you can write different tests using
LLM as a judge to ask a different LLM

474
00:29:25,031 --> 00:29:27,061
whether, how is my current AI doing?

475
00:29:28,111 --> 00:29:31,721
So in order to think of evals,
it's just like again your normal

476
00:29:31,721 --> 00:29:33,181
testing software pipeline.

477
00:29:33,841 --> 00:29:39,831
So you should have a regression evals
in your CI pipeline or a CD pipeline.

478
00:29:39,831 --> 00:29:44,221
So basically, whenever you're making
changes in your agent, it could be

479
00:29:44,831 --> 00:29:48,061
you are fine-tuning the temperature,
you are making changes in the system

480
00:29:48,061 --> 00:29:50,191
prompt, you are switching the model.

481
00:29:50,481 --> 00:29:51,361
It could be anything.

482
00:29:51,761 --> 00:29:58,661
So if you have a pre-quality gate before
it goes to production, you will be able to

483
00:29:58,701 --> 00:30:03,971
catch regressions much sooner than later
when you deploy it to your customers.

484
00:30:04,811 --> 00:30:06,111
Once it goes to production

485
00:30:06,561 --> 00:30:08,701
You should definitely
think about telemetry.

486
00:30:08,701 --> 00:30:11,111
When I say telemetry, like
when it goes to production

487
00:30:13,313 --> 00:30:19,063
It will-- The production is like you can
run evals on your logs of your agent.

488
00:30:19,493 --> 00:30:22,353
You can run evals on the
traces of your agent.

489
00:30:22,683 --> 00:30:25,693
Basically, you should definitely
capture what the agent is doing

490
00:30:25,703 --> 00:30:27,373
at every step in production.

491
00:30:27,893 --> 00:30:35,373
And again, throw an LLM as a judge to it
that the user is asking some question,

492
00:30:35,823 --> 00:30:41,723
and I am not answering according to the
question, or the user seems frustrated.

493
00:30:41,723 --> 00:30:46,213
For example, we have a very basic eval,
we call it user temperament, and we

494
00:30:46,213 --> 00:30:50,013
have a very basic system prompt to call
out, does the user seem frustrated?

495
00:30:50,153 --> 00:30:51,663
Is the user typing in caps?

496
00:30:52,193 --> 00:30:54,473
Is the user repeating the
same question again and again?

497
00:30:54,743 --> 00:30:58,793
So these are some of the signals you
should start with to hint whether

498
00:30:58,793 --> 00:31:00,393
your AI is working good or not.

499
00:31:00,883 --> 00:31:04,223
And then you can dig deeper more
into how you can test it better.

500
00:31:06,433 --> 00:31:08,233
And yeah, the idea is to shift left.

501
00:31:08,293 --> 00:31:11,453
The idea is to shift
left any testing you do.

502
00:31:11,753 --> 00:31:13,793
Again, be it AI, be it non-AI.

503
00:31:14,253 --> 00:31:14,413
Yep.

504
00:31:17,749 --> 00:31:21,649
The last piece for -- I'll start.

505
00:31:22,349 --> 00:31:27,919
So the last piece for evals where it
makes it different from unit tests is you

506
00:31:29,419 --> 00:31:32,819
-- Let's say you have hundred unit tests, and
you always want your hundred unit tests

507
00:31:32,819 --> 00:31:34,769
to pass whenever you're shipping software.

508
00:31:34,769 --> 00:31:38,559
If one of them fails, you go and fix it.

509
00:31:38,929 --> 00:31:41,159
It's a bit different with evals.

510
00:31:41,479 --> 00:31:46,319
You should think about evals as the
dataset you have where you are evaluating

511
00:31:46,369 --> 00:31:48,649
your AI, everything should not pass.

512
00:31:48,959 --> 00:31:53,339
The reason I say that is, if you're
improving your AI, you wouldn't

513
00:31:53,339 --> 00:31:54,459
know whether it is improving.

514
00:31:54,569 --> 00:31:58,899
So if you have seen all the benchmarks out
there in the world, like SWE benchmark,

515
00:31:58,899 --> 00:32:03,239
where all the models evaluate that we
are able to crack eighty-eight percent on

516
00:32:03,239 --> 00:32:04,999
the leaderboard out of hundred percent.

517
00:32:05,519 --> 00:32:08,419
That means they're not able to
pass hundred percent, but they

518
00:32:08,419 --> 00:32:11,359
are able to measure that I am at
eighty-eight percent right now.

519
00:32:11,929 --> 00:32:16,229
I make some changes to it so that
I would be able to know whether

520
00:32:16,229 --> 00:32:18,599
I'm going from eighty-eight to
eighty-seven or eighty-nine.

521
00:32:19,099 --> 00:32:21,259
So that's the difference
I'm trying to call out.

522
00:32:21,769 --> 00:32:26,329
If you have evals defined in a way that
you think of them as benchmarks, like

523
00:32:26,339 --> 00:32:31,369
those hundred unit test cases, out of
hundred, if eighty are passing, now

524
00:32:31,369 --> 00:32:36,369
anytime you're making changes to your
agent, if it is going from eighty to

525
00:32:36,369 --> 00:32:38,159
eighty-five, you know it is improving.

526
00:32:38,619 --> 00:32:41,579
If it is going from eighty to
seventy-five, you mean it is regressing.

527
00:32:41,679 --> 00:32:44,959
So that's the difference in
the mental model when you think

528
00:32:44,959 --> 00:32:46,839
about evals versus unit tests.

529
00:32:49,849 --> 00:32:52,869
Yeah, a benchmark without bad
tests cannot measure progress.

530
00:32:52,869 --> 00:32:56,709
It can only measure perfection
what you have configured before.

531
00:32:58,759 --> 00:33:00,189
The second piece is

532
00:33:02,205 --> 00:33:05,375
Whenever your evals are
failing, it means it's a signal.

533
00:33:05,825 --> 00:33:10,405
It means whenever your eval is
failing in production, whenever a

534
00:33:10,405 --> 00:33:12,425
user is frustrated, you get a signal.

535
00:33:12,905 --> 00:33:18,285
You get a particular data set, you get
a particular behavior where you have

536
00:33:18,285 --> 00:33:20,335
seen that agent is not working right.

537
00:33:20,885 --> 00:33:27,015
So you turn it into a feedback loop
so that you put that test in your CI

538
00:33:27,015 --> 00:33:30,245
now, so that now whenever next time
you are making changes to the agent,

539
00:33:30,255 --> 00:33:31,935
the same issue does not happen again.

540
00:33:32,595 --> 00:33:38,205
So it's like it's any failure
you are having in your production

541
00:33:38,205 --> 00:33:41,915
system, it becomes a new test
case, new eval test case.

542
00:33:42,355 --> 00:33:46,565
You add it to your benchmark, and you
are reaching towards a self-healing AI.

543
00:33:46,965 --> 00:33:50,755
It-- You are automatically improving your
AI, looking at failures in the production,

544
00:33:50,755 --> 00:33:53,135
and improving your benchmarks one by one.

545
00:33:58,241 --> 00:34:02,641
On to the last piece, the most boring
piece which a lot of guys skip,

546
00:34:03,041 --> 00:34:09,171
but the most important piece for
enterprise is kind-- kind-kind of

547
00:34:09,171 --> 00:34:10,981
divided into four different sections.

548
00:34:11,381 --> 00:34:12,061
Who did it?

549
00:34:12,381 --> 00:34:13,121
What happened?

550
00:34:13,411 --> 00:34:14,271
How did it happen?

551
00:34:14,591 --> 00:34:15,421
Why did it happen?

552
00:34:16,051 --> 00:34:22,731
So who did it is you're defining the
right access control on what an agent

553
00:34:22,731 --> 00:34:24,851
can do versus what an agent cannot do.

554
00:34:25,291 --> 00:34:29,981
I cannot just give it the entire set
of tools to the agent and ima- and

555
00:34:29,991 --> 00:34:31,581
just hope that it would work great.

556
00:34:32,081 --> 00:34:35,581
I would definitely want to
control what an agent can do.

557
00:34:35,581 --> 00:34:42,721
So for example, I gave it a GitHub MCP,
and GitHub MCP has some tools to create

558
00:34:42,721 --> 00:34:47,381
users, some tools to delete tokens, some
tools to delete branches or delete PRs.

559
00:34:47,791 --> 00:34:50,721
I shouldn't even give access to
all of those tools to my agent.

560
00:34:51,201 --> 00:34:56,891
So that is the one piece, basically
reducing the scope and the surface

561
00:34:56,921 --> 00:35:02,051
of access you're giving your agent,
basically defining who can do what.

562
00:35:03,361 --> 00:35:05,581
The second is the guardrails.

563
00:35:05,971 --> 00:35:09,061
How do you protect what data
is used and where it flows?

564
00:35:09,531 --> 00:35:12,311
So there are different types
of guardrails that exist.

565
00:35:12,801 --> 00:35:18,092
First, starting from the top is the
PII detect- detection and reduction.

566
00:35:18,231 --> 00:35:20,491
So if

567
00:35:23,132 --> 00:35:27,092
for e- for example, if you're talking
about what is my Aadhaar number, what is

568
00:35:27,092 --> 00:35:31,742
my email ID, what is my address, should
not be sending all of these sensitive

569
00:35:31,742 --> 00:35:36,891
data to LLMs because enterprises care
a lot about what sensitive data are

570
00:35:36,932 --> 00:35:38,872
being sent to LLM versus what is not.

571
00:35:39,561 --> 00:35:43,342
So this comes under the right privacy
guardrails, the right data guardrails,

572
00:35:44,362 --> 00:35:46,121
the right data residency guardrails.

573
00:35:46,552 --> 00:35:50,972
Data residency, by data res- residency
for example, a very good example is if,

574
00:35:51,022 --> 00:35:53,041
let's say you have a customer in Europe.

575
00:35:53,611 --> 00:35:55,461
Europe has a very strict compliance.

576
00:35:55,921 --> 00:36:00,111
You cannot send the data out
from Europe to any other region.

577
00:36:00,511 --> 00:36:05,092
So I should make sure that all the
data I'm talking with AI is only

578
00:36:05,092 --> 00:36:10,081
being accessed from the EU ecosystem
and not from outside of EU ecosystem.

579
00:36:12,648 --> 00:36:16,568
The third piece is enforcing
how AI behaves in real time.

580
00:36:17,258 --> 00:36:22,678
Some content safety filters, some
toxicness fil-filters things like those.

581
00:36:23,258 --> 00:36:27,567
And the last piece is even if you
do all of this, let's say something

582
00:36:27,567 --> 00:36:31,567
happened, something you're trying
to debug an incident, or you're

583
00:36:31,567 --> 00:36:36,907
trying to figure out why it happened,
who caused it, and all of it.

584
00:36:37,357 --> 00:36:41,537
If you don't have the right
auditability, what is happening, if

585
00:36:41,537 --> 00:36:46,508
you don't have the right traceability,
you won't be able to debug it back

586
00:36:46,517 --> 00:36:48,947
on who did it, how it happened.

587
00:36:48,987 --> 00:36:49,937
Did agent do it?

588
00:36:50,007 --> 00:36:50,827
Did you do it?

589
00:36:51,267 --> 00:36:52,278
How did agent do it?

590
00:36:52,667 --> 00:36:58,267
So you should definitely think about
full audit trails of what the actions

591
00:36:58,267 --> 00:37:02,048
agent is taking, what are the different
model versions it is using at that

592
00:37:02,067 --> 00:37:07,128
time, what are the different tool
calls the agent is making, and comply

593
00:37:07,128 --> 00:37:10,307
to all of the different compliance
systems that exist in the world.

594
00:37:11,107 --> 00:37:14,877
Again, with the code, ending with
the code is context is the new code,

595
00:37:14,927 --> 00:37:21,028
governance is the new runtime because
governance is only applied at runtime.

596
00:37:21,028 --> 00:37:23,507
Governance is not defined
when you're writing code.

597
00:37:23,577 --> 00:37:27,317
Governance is only applied when your
agents are running, and you have to

598
00:37:27,798 --> 00:37:28,837
put the right guardrails at runtime.

599
00:37:32,220 --> 00:37:37,699
Just to summarize the entire enterprise
AI stack, starting with context, the new

600
00:37:37,719 --> 00:37:40,149
code defines what AI knows, why it acts.

601
00:37:40,489 --> 00:37:45,009
The first most important piece for your AI
to not hallucinate, to keep it grounded,

602
00:37:45,619 --> 00:37:47,879
and with the knowledge graph and all.

603
00:37:48,539 --> 00:37:50,039
The knowledge graph is the compiler.

604
00:37:50,039 --> 00:37:53,539
It translates your complete
organization knowledge into a

605
00:37:53,539 --> 00:37:55,029
usable structure like a graph.

606
00:37:55,319 --> 00:37:55,979
So

607
00:37:58,059 --> 00:38:00,129
it heavily helps in the
context engineering.

608
00:38:00,129 --> 00:38:04,269
Instead of AI figuring it out, you throw
a graph, you throw a query layer at it,

609
00:38:04,649 --> 00:38:06,779
and it gets better at querying your data.

610
00:38:07,919 --> 00:38:09,709
The third is the governance.

611
00:38:09,929 --> 00:38:10,750
The AI is running.

612
00:38:10,849 --> 00:38:12,039
How do you govern it?

613
00:38:12,109 --> 00:38:15,590
How do you make sure that it is
not going rogue, it is not bringing

614
00:38:15,590 --> 00:38:17,039
down your production databases?

615
00:38:17,839 --> 00:38:21,250
And the last piece, the most
important piece when you are def--

616
00:38:21,329 --> 00:38:23,309
building AI is the testing of AI.

617
00:38:23,339 --> 00:38:25,409
How do you measure trust, performance?

618
00:38:26,029 --> 00:38:31,009
How do you know your AI is failing in
production and users are facing issues?

619
00:38:31,019 --> 00:38:34,379
Because you cannot build an AI, the
most perfect AI in the first shot.

620
00:38:34,789 --> 00:38:38,359
It will work fine in demos, it will
f- work fine on, on your laptops,

621
00:38:38,389 --> 00:38:42,210
it will work fine on your QA, but it
might not work fine on production.

622
00:38:42,559 --> 00:38:46,429
So it, it should always be
a continuous feedback loop.

623
00:38:46,589 --> 00:38:52,049
You build AI, you deploy it, let the
regression test run, you ship it,

624
00:38:52,219 --> 00:38:56,689
you constantly watch it, see what
is failing, come back as a feedback

625
00:38:56,690 --> 00:38:59,999
loop, fix it, then keep doing that.

626
00:38:59,999 --> 00:39:04,260
So it's like you complete the entire
circle of building your AI, and

627
00:39:04,699 --> 00:39:08,929
you will eventually reach a stage
where it is like a self-healing AI.

628
00:39:11,637 --> 00:39:12,137
That's it.

629
00:39:12,237 --> 00:39:13,107
That's it from my side.

630
00:39:13,237 --> 00:39:13,718
Thank, thanks a lot

